        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The objective of the experiment was to assess the redistribution of fluid volumes, including losses and changes in composition and pressure as influenced by the loss of hydrostatic forces during space flight.  The investigators wanted to obtain information about body fluid regulation under operational Shuttle conditions.  Skylab and ASTP data had shown that a major fluid volume shift occurred before flight day 3(FD3), but the time course of the shift was still not understood. Measurements of changes in leg volume were made pre- and postflight on Apollo 16 and 17 and subsequently on Skylab 2, 3, and 4.  The volume measurement was accomplished by using a modification of a measurement system for fitting support stockings. Single-point calf girth measurements were also done in flight on Skylab 2, 3, and 4. The postflight leg volume measurements made on these missions were late in the recovery period after the return of fluid was largely over. On Skylab 4, in-flight changes in leg volume were documented using a tape measure.  A repeat of the leg volume study was performed on the Apollo-Soyuz Test Project (ASTP) system.<P>
Previous space flights had also suggested that the weightless environment presented significant challenges to man's ability to maintain homeostasis, and included weight loss, increased aldosterone secretion, and loss of sodium and potassium.  Furthermore, it was hypothesized that some of the factors involved in fluid metabolism changes were also involved in the space adaptation syndrome (SAS).
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
The tape measure methodology used in the Skylab and ASTP studies was tedious and time consuming. Because of this, it was not practical to make measurements early on-orbit or at recovery when crew time was limited; however, these were the times when the fluid shift was most critical.  There wasn't an opportunity to repeat the studies until the Space Shuttle became operational.  Other methods of volume determination, such as water displacement, were impractical in the weightless environment of space or were logistically difficult and time and equipment intensive.   A simpler, much more rapid scheme for obtaining volumes was therefore devised and resulted in the stocking plethysmograph used during the Shuttle program.  <P>
The method was first tested on STS-5, and then used on STS-7 and 8, then later routinely used on Shuttle flights.  On STS-5, a plastic marking window was used.  It was found that it did not produce uniform friction on the measuring tapes, thus introducing significant errors in measurements.  A uniform and consistent metal marking window was designed and used on STS-7 and 8.  Prior to STS-7, a series of repeatability and validity tests were run to compare the stockings to a tape measure.  In addition, the stocking method was compared to the water displacement method.  Details about the design of the stocking plethysmograph can be found in Moore TP, Thornton WE. Space shuttle inflight and postflight fluid shifts measured by leg volume changes. Aviation, Space, and Environmental Medicine. 1987.
<P>
On STS-8, urine collections were done for 3 days preflight, in 12 hour pools throughout the mission, and for 3 days after the flight to determine the time course of the metabolic changes.  Urine was collected in bags containing lithium chloride and boric acid.  Postflight assays of lithium concentration gave the total void volume, and boric acid served as the preservative.  Specimens collected in flight could not be frozen, so aliquots of the pre- and post-flight specimens were kept at room temperature for the duration of the mission and were compared to specimens frozen immediately, to determine the effect of storage conditions.  Samples were analyzed for volume, electrolytes, cortisol, aldosterone and antidiuretic hormone (ADH).
<P>
Records of food and fluid intake were also kept.  Approximately 2 hours before landing, a blood sample was drawn into a heparinized tube and was analyzed immediately postflight.
<P>
RESULTS:<br>
The earliest inflight stocking plethysmograph data from STS-7 and 8 was obtained at 11 and 13 hours Mission Elapsed Time (MET).  The conclusion was that by the this time inflight, the fluid shift from the legs was essentially complete, since later inflight measurements showed no significant further leg volume loss.  As much as 4 liters of fluid shifted from the legs within the first few hours of flight.
<p>
On STS-8, excretion of ADH and then cortisol increased greatly during the first 24 hours of flight but returned to levels only slightly above normal on the second day.  Later the excretion rate of urine volume and then aldosterone increased.  The results indicate that homeostatic mechanisms are indeed affected by flight conditions.
<P>
This experiment was extended to subsequent missions (as DSOs 446 and 461) to further understand the time course of the fluid shift.  Results from all 3 experiments can be found under LSDA records for DSOs 446 and 461.
